// Package otlpreceiver records model calls that clients report over OpenTelemetry instead of sending through relay. It serves the OTLP/HTTP export endpoints for traces and logs: each exported span or log record that a registered convention recognises as a model call becomes a usage event attributed to the authenticated caller and priced from the catalog. When the operator turns content capture on, the message content a client reports with a call is stored as the event's payload record.
//
// Reported usage is self-declared, so nothing here is an enforcement point: no policy grant or key pool is consulted, and every event carries the source "otlp" so readers can tell it from traffic relay carried. The one limit applied is the rate limit on export requests, which protects the receiver itself. Records that are not model calls are not stored. The metrics signal is out of scope.
//
// Exporters retry, and a client may report one call as a span and as an event, so a call is identified by its reporter's project (or principal), trace id and span id and recorded once: an export that cannot be queued whole is refused and sent again, and the calls already recorded are skipped. Content is marked separately from usage, so content that arrives after the usage row is still stored, once. Whether content is stored at all is the operator's decision, not the client's: a policy that governs the reporter must allow payload logging, and only a reporter with no policy has what it sends kept. The usage and payload queues are shared with proxied traffic, which drops records when they are full, so reported records are queued only while a queue is under half full.
//
// Expected kv ops per export: 1 RunScript for the rate limit reservation, and 1 batched round trip marking the export's calls (one script call per distinct trace id, pipelined when the store batches). An export carrying content while capture is on adds 1 batched round trip marking that content. A further batched round trip removes markers only when a queue filled while the export was being queued.
package otlpreceiver
